The binding hands the submission out as a Lua table, and the built-in assistant module (k109) serialises those values back to JSON in Lua - a hand-written jstr/jval in the module source. The house has a JSON reader in C (src/net/json.c) but no writer, and the module's writer is not the house's bounded one: it is unbounded in depth except for its own depth > 4 guard, and it replaces control characters with '?'. Two ways out: the binding hands out the submission's own JSON bytes instead of a table (the core built them already, so they are the contract), or the core grows a bounded JSON writer the module uses. The first is smaller. Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k109.

Binding side done (approach a, the smaller one): canlin.view.submission() now hands out the submission's own JSON bytes instead of a Lua table. src/assistant/view.c: canlin_assistant_view_take_lua -> canlin_assistant_view_take_json (the table machinery is deleted, no writer in Lua); include/canlin/assistant_view.h and include/canlin/assistant_binding.h document the new contract; src/assistant/binding.c calls it. t/assistant/test_binding.c pins it: the module receives the core's own bytes (compared with the submission the C side takes) and the values survive, the graph's nodes and ports included - the old Lua writer dropped them at depth > 4. Red/green shown: 19 of 130 checks failed against the old binding, all pass now. Host suite green: make BUILD=build/llm4 test -> ALL TESTS PASSED.

Still open in the platform lane (src/app/assistant.c, not touched here): delete the jstr/jval (lines 49-88) and make line 224 turn(sub) instead of turn('action=' .. tostring(sub.action) .. ' values=' .. jval(sub.values, 1)). Until that hunk lands the module sends 'action=nil values=null' to the provider (indexing a string yields nil, no error). t/app/test_assistant.c does not catch it: its 'Milch' check is satisfied by the set_view tool-call arguments, so it needs a check on the submission's values in the request body.

Nachbesserung: der Vertrag von canlin_assistant_view_take_json() ist im Header vollstaendig. include/canlin/assistant_view.h nennt alle drei Faelle: CANLIN_AV_OK (die Bytes liegen als Lua-String auf dem Stack), CANLIN_AV_ESTALE (es wartet keine Submission, nichts auf dem Stack), CANLIN_AV_EARG (keine View oder kein Lua-State, nichts auf dem Stack). Der Kommentar in src/assistant/view.c nennt dieselben drei Faelle.

Zur Frage nach dem Code: EARG bekommt keinen eigenen Modul-Code. CANLIN_AV_EARG ist C API misuse (include/canlin/assistant_view.h:75) - ein Fehler des Aufrufers, kein Zustand, den ein Modul ausloesen oder verstehen kann, und keiner der Modul-Codes der core API spec §3.1. Das Haus mappt es selbst so: canlin_av_code() (src/assistant/view.c:80-89) schickt jeden ungenannten Status nach invalid, EARG eingeschlossen. Der default-Zweig in src/assistant/binding.c ist also die Hausregel, nicht eine Vermutung; der Kommentar dort sagt es jetzt ausdruecklich. Zudem ist EARG vom Binding aus nicht erreichbar: die Installation verweigert ein Binding ohne View (src/assistant/binding.c:79-80), und L ist der laufende Zustand.

Ergaenzt: t/assistant/test_view.c hat test_take_json_earg() - der Missbrauch wird verweigert (nichts auf dem Stack). Host: make BUILD=build/llm4 test -> ALL TESTS PASSED (assistant view 1925 checks, assistant binding 130, assistant response 3582, app assistant 286). src/app/assistant.c und src/shell/ unberuehrt; die App-Haelfte liegt als k137 im Backlog.

